<Project>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      MinVer derives the version from the git tag (see Directory.Build.props). Build-time only:
      Directory.Build.props references it with PrivateAssets="all", so it appears in no nuspec.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="MinVer" Version="7.0.0" />

    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.DevServer" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly.Server" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.FluentUI.AspNetCore.Components" Version="4.14.4" />
    <!--
      A RANGE, NOT A POINT, AND IT IS THE ONE NUMBER CONSUMERS INHERIT. NuGet reads a bare
      `Version="10.0.0"` in a nuspec as ">= 10.0.0" with no ceiling, so the package would resolve
      against EF Core 11. This provider is built on EF internals by design (19 files carry
      `#pragma warning disable EF1001`), and a major EF release moves them, so an unbounded range
      trades a clear restore error for a runtime failure inside IStateManager.

      Npgsql declares the same shape: its 10.0.0 is [10.0.0, 11.0.0) and its 10.0.2 is
      [10.0.4, 11.0.0), the floor raised deliberately once it needed a later patch.

      THE FLOOR IS THE POINT OF THE LOWER BOUND. It is what a consumer is forced onto, so do not
      raise it to chase a patch; raise it only when this provider needs a fix that shipped in one.
      .github/dependabot.yml ignores this family for exactly that reason.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="[10.0.0,11.0.0)" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Proxies" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational.Specification.Tests" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Specification.Tests" Version="10.0.0" />
    <!--
      10.0.9, not 10.0.0. This entry is a TRANSITIVE PIN, not a dependency of the product:
      CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled makes it bind every transitive reference in the repo,
      and EF Core's SQLite package reaches Microsoft.Extensions.Logging 10.0.9, which floors this
      at 10.0.9. Pinned lower, the restore fails with NU1109 in the test and sample projects.
      Raising it changes no declared dependency of InfoCarrier.Core, which references only
      Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore and Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational.

      CORRECTED 2026-08-17 (M8-19), then CORRECTED AGAIN 2026-08-25, and the second correction
      withdraws the first. M8-19 said transitive pinning promotes this pin into the nuspec, so the
      package would declare Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection >= 10.0.9 although no csproj
      references it. It does not. Read out of a packed nuspec on 2026-08-25:

        <dependency id="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" version="10.0.0" exclude="Build,Analyzers" />

      That is the whole dependency group. The pin is a build-time floor and nothing more, so no
      dependency nobody chose reaches a consumer. The floor itself is still real and still needed:
      EF Core's SQLite package reaches 10.0.9, and turning CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled
      off for the packable projects reintroduces the NU1109 this pin exists to prevent.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" Version="10.0.9" />
    <!--
      TRANSITIVE PIN THAT CLOSES NU1903 ADVISORIES. Not referenced directly by anything here and
      does not reach either shipped package (`dotnet list package` with the vulnerable switch
      reports InfoCarrier.Core and InfoCarrier.Core.AspNetCore clean); it arrives behind
      Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer / Microsoft.Data.SqlClient, reached ONLY by
      InfoCarrier.Core.FunctionalTests. A version floor rather than a suppression because the fix
      is a clean minor bump.

      System.Security.Cryptography.Xml 9.0.0 to 10.0.11 (EIGHT high advisories: GHSA-23rf-6693-g89p,
      GHSA-37gx-xxp4-5rgx, GHSA-6588-8gv4-xfgh, GHSA-8q5v-6pqq-x66h, GHSA-cvvh-rhrc-wg4q,
      GHSA-g8r8-53c2-pm3f, GHSA-mmjf-rqrv-855v, GHSA-w3x6-4m5h-cxqf). 10.0.x rather than a 9.0.x
      patch because everything targets net10.0.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="System.Security.Cryptography.Xml" Version="10.0.11" />

    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="18.9.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="4.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="System.Text.Json" Version="10.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="coverlet.collector" Version="10.0.1" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
